{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Correction to the close-class named at the end of the previous comment: closing\n`--class plan_defect`, not `regression`.\n\nReasoning against the bounded vocabulary in `docs/ticket-model.md`:\n\n- `plan_defect` (\"the design/plan itself was wrong\") — CHOSEN. The op-cert key-custody design\n  gave the SIGN side three resolution sources (bound startup signer, `REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH`,\n  tracker genesis) but specified only ONE for the VERIFY side (`<tracker>/.opcert-key.pub`).\n  The two halves were never designed to share a chain, which is precisely what 2337 corrected.\n- `regression` — REJECTED: it means a change reintroduced a previously-FIXED defect. This\n  mismatch was never fixed before; it was latent from the moment the write side gained\n  resolution branches the read side did not mirror.\n- `env_integration` — REJECTED: the deployment (a read-only bind-mounted secret, a tracker with\n  no keypair) only EXPOSED the defect. The fault and the fix are both in rebar's own code\n  (`src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py`), so this is not an environment or integration issue.\n- `preexisting` — REJECTED as less precise: the defect was indeed latent, but naming it\n  `plan_defect` records WHY it was latent, which is the useful signal for the follow-up\n  `879b-9bf0-86fd-4a6b` guards."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787959105093431001,"uuid":"1c6eff89-3e06-4490-a633-6c4377ff2281"}